course: dictionary
lesson: 15655
title: A formal word: thereof
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to official papers, not to a chat with a friend. Thereof is one of them. You will most often meet it in rules, notices and legal forms, where writers want to be exact. Read the words around it to find its meaning: «Mia read the club rules, every part thereof.» Mia read every part of the rules, so thereof means of them.
explain: Use the swap test. Put «of it» or «of that» in place of thereof and check that the sentence still makes sense. «Any part thereof is free» becomes «any part of it is free», and that works, so thereof means of it. The word points back to something that was just mentioned, so look for that thing earlier in the text.
fact: Thereof is a formal word, mostly found in legal papers, official notices and rules.
fact: Thereof means of that, of it, or of those just mentioned.
fact: «Any part thereof» means any part of it.
fact: You may spot phrases like «or part thereof» and «lack thereof» in official writing.
exercise: «Mia read the club rules, every part thereof.» What does thereof mean here? | Far away, over the hill | *Of the rules just mentioned | Of a sandwich | Very loudly
exercise: Which phrase means the same as «a proof thereof»? | on Tuesday morning | *a proof of it | under the table | in a hurry
exercise: Where would you most likely meet the word thereof? | A text message to a friend | A football score | A birthday card | *An official notice or set of rules
test: «The form asked for a receipt or proof thereof.» What does thereof mean here? | Very old | *Of it, meaning the payment | In the garden | A kind of pen
test: Which phrase means the same as «any part thereof»? | *any part of it | any part for a birthday | any part, very quickly | any part of the sky
exam: «The notice warned of a fine, and the lack thereof would be checked.» What does thereof mean in this sentence? | *Of that, meaning the fine | A bright light | A loud noise | A huge crowd
